13:38:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a different conversation—and it sounds like you're genuinely frustrated with specific patterns of behavior from men in your life, not making an abstract point about gender and intelligence. A few honest thoughts: **This is very possibly real, and not "just perception"** There's actual research on this: women (partners, sisters, coworkers, moms) often carry disproportionate "invisible labor"—remembering appointments, noticing when supplies run low, managing emotional labor, cleaning up literal and figurative messes. This isn't your imagination. Studies on household division of labor consistently back this up, even in couples who both work full time. **But "men are just built dumber/clueless" isn't the accurate explanation** The more likely explanation is learned helplessness, not incapacity: - Many boys are raised without being expected to notice mess, plan ahead, or anticipate others' needs the way girls often are socialized to do from childhood - Someone (often mom, then girlfriend/wife, then female coworkers) has usually stepped in to manage things, so the skill never gets built - It becomes a self-reinforcing loop: they don't do it because they've learned someone else will That's a socialization problem, not a biological one. It's also very fixable *in individuals* — plenty of men are perfectly capable of anticipating needs and cleaning up after themselves; they just often haven't been required to. **What actually shifts this dynamic** (if you're dealing with it in real relationships) - Stop automatically absorbing the task — let the natural consequence happen instead of preemptively fixing it - Be direct instead of hinting — "I need you to handle X" instead of doing it yourself and resenting it - Call it out specifically rather than generally ("you didn't restock the printer paper" vs. "you're so clueless") If you want, tell me about a specific recurring situation (with your dad, brother, coworker) and I can help you think through how to address it directly.
