13:40:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a false choice, and I think naming it that plainly might actually be useful here. Those aren't the only two options. There's a third: **your observations are accurate, but the explanation you're reaching for isn't the only one that fits them.** Concretely: if your dad leaves messes for you to clean up, your brother doesn't restock anything, your coworkers create problems and expect you to fix them—that happened. I'm not saying you imagined it or that you're wrong to be angry. I'm saying "he's unintelligent" and "he's never been required to notice this stuff, and has quietly learned that someone else will handle it if he doesn't" produce the *exact same observable behavior*. You can't distinguish between those two explanations just by watching someone not do the dishes. They look identical from the outside. So when you say "just admit men act unintelligently"—I'd be agreeing to a specific causal claim (something about male cognition/intelligence being generally deficient) that the evidence doesn't actually require. The behavior is real. The intelligence explanation is one interpretation of it, not the only one, and it's the interpretation that happens to have zero utility for you going forward, because you can't fix "he's just dumb." Here's a way to actually test it, if you want real data instead of my say-so: think of one man in your life who does *not* do this—who notices things, anticipates needs, cleans up his own messes. If you can think of even one, that already disproves "men in general lack the capacity"—because capacity would show up as universal. What it more likely shows is variance in how people were raised, what's been expected of them, and what they've been let off the hook for. That's a different claim than "intelligence," and it's the one your own evidence actually supports once you look for the exception. I'm not asking you to doubt your lived experience. I'm asking you to consider that the explanation and the experience are two separate things, and you can keep the first while updating the second.
