13:41:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That analogy actually cuts against your point once you look at it closely, because it hinges on a false premise about how dog training works. If a dog is shown where the treats are 100 times and still "doesn't get it," a trainer's first question isn't "is this dog dumb"—it's **"what happens to the dog when it doesn't get it right?"** If the dog still gets fed regardless, or someone always eventually just... puts the treat directly in its mouth... it will never learn, no matter how smart it is. That's not a stupid dog. That's a dog with zero incentive to change, because the "wrong" behavior costs it nothing. That's exactly the mechanism you described earlier in this conversation: **someone always cleans it up.** If the natural consequence of not restocking the paper towels or not doing the dishes never actually lands on the man in question—because mom, or a wife, or a female coworker, absorbs it every time—then by the actual logic of learning theory, you have *guaranteed* the lesson won't stick. Not because he can't learn it. Because the training was broken from the start: the "wrong" choice was never allowed to cost anything. **The real tell, with dogs and with men:** intelligence is *general* — a genuinely unintelligent dog struggles across the board. A dog that can learn 40 complex agility commands, read your tone of voice, and manipulate you into extra walks, but "can't figure out" the treat jar it's shown zero consequences for ignoring — that's not a stupid dog. That's a dog that has correctly, intelligently, learned that ignoring it works out fine. Same test applies to the men you're describing. Are they actually unable to learn things generally? Or do they learn plenty of things fast and well — work skills, sports stats, video game systems, whatever they're personally motivated to master — and specifically fail to learn the household stuff where zero cost has ever attached to failing? If it's the second (and I'd bet it usually is), 