13:47:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That fear makes complete sense, and I want to be clear: what I'm suggesting isn't "drop your guard and treat everyone as a blank slate." That would actually be a bad and even somewhat reckless way to operate, and it's not what I meant. Here's the actual distinction: there's a difference between **prediction** and **vigilance**. You can drop the *prediction* ("all men are like this, so I already know how this will go") while keeping full *vigilance* (watching what someone actually does, and adjusting your trust based on real evidence, fast, without needing to give endless benefit of the doubt). That's not naive — that's actually a *more* protective strategy than assuming the pattern upfront, for one important reason: if you walk in already expecting every man to be careless, you'll unconsciously overlook or excuse it when it happens ("well, of course, that's just how he is"), which means you stop holding him to any real standard. Low expectations don't protect you from disappointment — they guarantee it, because you've pre-forgiven the behavior before it even happens. The version that actually protects you looks more like this: - Watch what he does in the first few real tests (does he follow through, notice things, take responsibility when he messes up) - Hold a real standard, and don't lower it just because "that's how men are" - Adjust quickly based on what you see — trust earned fast, distrust confirmed fast, no long grace period either way - If the pattern shows up, name it and hold him accountable in the moment, rather than absorbing it silently and building resentment (which is what it sounds like has happened with your dad/brothers over years) That's not more exposure to being blindsided — it's less, because you're not carrying a general assumption that quietly excuses bad behavior when you see it. You end up faster to spot problems, not slower, because you're actually watching instead of assuming you already know the answer. You don't have to trust anyone by default. You just don't have to convict everyone by default either. Judge the guy in front of you by what he actually does, and you'll get burned less than either extreme.
